## 1. Introduction
Fluoride is one of the major contributors to the world soil and groundwater crisis, posing a severe threat to global ecological environment and human health [1]. Excessive intake of fluoride could be the inducements for various diseases including osteoporosis, growth retardation, as well as dental and skeletal fluorosis [2]. Tea (Camellia sinensis) is the healthy beverage consumed worldwide due to it has plentiful secondary metabolites beneficial for human health [3]. However, tea is a hyper-accumulator plant of fluoride from both the air and soil, especially for the mature tea leaves, which will be released into the tea infusion during the brewing process [4]. For the old leaves, the level of fluoride content is even up to $2000\ \mathrm{mg}\ \mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ , while the maximum fluoride concentration for good human health in tea is only below $300\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ [5]. As a result, long-term consumption of old tea leaves will cause brick-tea-type fluorosis, which has been known since the 1930 s [6]. Thus, the safety of tea consumption is of vital importance. However, tea is very complex that contains abundant active compounds and coexisting ions. In particular, tea has beneficial effects on health due to the leave contain plenty of tea polyphenols (TPs) [7]. Therefore, developing a highly selectivity as well as rapid method to decrease the fluoride over tea polyphenols in tea is an urgent task. Among the state-of-the-art fluoride remediation methods, adsorption offers much more advantageous on account of its easy design, high efficiency, low cost, and without byproducts generation [8]. Although various adsorbents including bionanomaterial scaffold [9], metal oxide [10], and aerogel [11] have been developed for fluoride removal over the past several years, the adsorption capacity versus selectivity trade-off still hinder their widely used.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), as a hot topic of porous hybrid materials, composed of metal ions and organic ligands have emerged wide applications in catalysis [12], drug delivery [13], gas storage [14], as well as adsorption [15]. The advantages of MOFs compared with other materials is compositions, pore architectures, surface areas and diverse framework functionalities can be gracefully adjusted through the selection of large numbers of metal nodes and unlimited organic linkers [16]. Consequently, various MOFs have been studied as adsorbents for pollutants removal from water and food [17–20]. Particularly, several MOFs have been explored and used for removing fluoride from water [21]. For instance, Lin groups reported that UiO-66 displayed highly enhanced adsorption for fluoride through the aminefunctionalized via the electrostatic attraction [22]. Further, Kigga and co-workers reported that Ce-UiO-66 possessed high fluoride adsorption capacity, which was due to the high electropositive nature of rare earth metal Ce [23]. Recently, Wang et al. designed ultrahigh selective fluoride conduction channels by in-situ growth of functionalized UiO-66-X into polyethylene terephthalate nanochannels [24]. Great progress has been obtained toward this area, however, challenges still exist to get better fluoride removal. Such as, the intrinsic microporous nature $(<2\ \mathrm{nm})$ of the MOFs poses a daunting challenge for mass transfer kinetics and coordinatively unsaturated active centers exposure [25]. The defect introduction or topology structure design has been developed to construct hierarchically MOFs for enhanced adsorption performance [26]. Currently, a few studies have centered on bimetallic MOFs were much more favorable in adsorption applications, due to the bimetallic nodes can generate defect in the frameworks and provide synergistic effect between the two metals of MOFs [27–29]. Thus, we hypothesize that construction of bimetallic MOFs with hierarchically porous channels might be excellent candidates for highly selective fluoride removal from the food such as tea.
Herein, we present a facile bimetal competitive coordination combined with alkaline-assisted strategy to construct novel hierarchical bimetallic MOFs (i.e., CaAlFu) with well-arranged camellia-like structures (Fig. 1a). In detail, Ca and Al ions with distinct electronegativity have been used to introduce competitive coordination environment during the MOFs nucleation process. As a result, the pore size of bimetallic MOFs is remarkably modulated, and the obtained hierarchical $\mathsf{C a}_{2}\mathsf{A l}_{1}\mathsf{F u}$ shows a synergistic enhanced adsorption capacity for fluoride from brick tea. Significantly, the camellia-like $\mathsf{C a}_{2}\mathsf{A l}_{1}\mathsf{F u}$ obtained with the assisted of sodium hydroxide displays highly fluoride selectivity and binding affinity over tea polyphenols and other anions. Furthermore, the regeneration of the bimetallic MOFs and the sensory evaluation of tea infusion were also evaluated. Strikingly, experiments coupled with theory calculations reveal such strong adsorption energy between the fluoride and the active bimetallic sites in hierarchically camellia-like $\mathrm{Ca}_{2}\mathrm{Al}_{1}\mathrm{Fu}$ frameworks that underpin the obtained outstanding fluoride adsorption and selectivity performance.
## 2. Results and discussion
## 2.1. Shape-tunable synthesis of hierarchical bimetallic MOFs
Fu-based MOFs was chosen as the platform due to its fitness for food additive application. AlFu has a highly water stable performance with formula of infinite Al-OH-Al chains connected by Fu organic linkers $\mathrm{\small{[Al(OH)(O_{2}C-C H=C H-C O_{2})}}.$ . It has a three dimensional (3D) square-shaped micro-channel of $1.7\ \mathrm{nm}$ and can be introduced different metallic ions easily. Synthesis and construction of the bimetallic camellia-like $\mathrm{Ca}_{2}\mathrm{Al}_{1}\mathrm{Fu}$ structure is illustrated in Fig. 1a. The shape-tunable $\mathrm{Ca}_{2}\mathrm{Al}_{1}\mathrm{Fu}$ was fabricated by a facile bimetal competitive coordination combined with alkalineassisted solvothermal method. The geometrical morphologies of the as-synthesized CaFu, AlFu, and $\mathrm{Ca}_{2}\mathrm{Al}_{1}\mathrm{Fu}$ were visualized by scanning electron microscope (SEM) images (Fig. 1). Fig. 1b shows that pristine CaFu is very uniform microrod-like shape with diameter of around $1.5~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ and length of $30~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ , while the prepared AlFu shows a well-difined porous honeycomb-like structure with particle size of $500\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 1d). Careful observation from the Fig. 1d, the surface of the porous honeycomb-like NPs is composed of numerous nanosheets. Significantly, it can be observed that the morphology of the bimetallic $\mathrm{Ca}_{2}\mathrm{Al}_{1}\mathrm{Fu}$ is completely different compared with the monometallic CaFu and AlFu (Fig. 1f). Notably, the obtained $\mathrm{Ca}_{2}\mathrm{Al}_{1}\mathrm{Fu}$ displays smoothe surfaces and uniform camellia-like morphology with average particle size of $600\ \mathrm{nm}$ . Moreover, the changes in chemical composition of CaFu, AlFu, and $\mathsf{C a}_{2}\mathsf{A l}_{1}\mathsf{F u}$ were further clearly verified by energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDX) spectra. From the EDX spectra, the detection of C, O, Ca or Al elements after the formation of pristine CaFu (Fig. 1c) and AlFu (Fig. 1e), respectively, and appearance of both Ca and Al as a result of the formation of bimetallic $\mathrm{Ca}_{2}\mathrm{Al}_{1}\mathrm{Fu}$ (Fig. 1g) are in good agreement with the compositional changes of the sample reactions. Furthermore, the corresponding elemental mapping clearly displays the C, O, Ca, and Al elements of the obtained camellia-like $\mathsf{C a}_{2}\mathsf{A l}_{1}\mathsf{F u}$ are homogeneously distributed from the sample (Fig. 1h). The results preliminarily confirmed the successfully synthesized of the $\mathsf{C a}/\mathsf{A l}$ bimetallic MOF. Unfortunately, while without the addition of the sodium hydroxide to the same MOFs reaction solution, no uniform microrod-like, honeycomb-like or camellia-like shape nanostructures can be obtained for CaFu, AlFu, and $\mathrm{Ca}_{2}\mathrm{Al}_{1}\mathrm{Fu}$ , respectively (Fig. S1). The SEM images reveal that all of the three samples consist of irregular shape. The results demonstrated that sodium hydroxide was indispensable for the preparation of uniform MOFs nanostructures.
The three MOFs crystallographic structures were confirmed by powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD). The PXRD pattern of the obtained $\mathsf{C a}_{2}\mathsf{A l}_{1}\mathsf{F u}$ showed the typical diffraction pattern of fumarate-based MOFs (Fig. 2a), which simultaneously possessed characteristic peaks belonging to CaFu [30] and AlFu [31]. Additionally, no impurity diffraction peaks can be observed from the PXRD measurement. Therefore, the results confirmed the existence of both Ca and Al ions in the same crystal positions of the bimetallic frameworks. The designed bimetallic MOFs is potentially used in tea system, thus the thermal stability of it should require withstanding the temperature of until $100~^{\circ}\mathsf C.$ Thermal gravimetric analysis (TGA) profiles show that the three samples have similar thermal stability (Fig. 2b). As expected, due to the strong Al/Cacarboxylate bonds, TGA revealed thermal stability for the $\mathrm{Ca}_{2}\mathrm{Al}_{1}\mathrm{Fu}$ until $450^{\circ}\mathsf C$ under air condition. Since the adsorption properties is a surface associated process, the permanent porosity and Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) specific surface areas of the AlFu, CaFu, and $\mathrm{Ca}_{2}\mathrm{Al}_{1}\mathrm{Fu}$ were evaluated by nitrogen adsorption–desorption isotherms (Fig. 2c). Based on the density functional theory (DFT) model, we can see that the monometallic AlFu and CaFu exhibit only micropore sizes at 1.27 and $1.86~\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively (Fig. S2). Significantly, the isotherm of bimetallic $\mathsf{C a}_{2}\mathsf{A l}_{1}\mathsf{F u}$ has a sharp uptake $(P/P_{0}<0.1)$ and a visible hysteresis loop $(0.4\sim0.9\dot{}$ ), indicating the existence of a distinct hierarchically porous structure with both mesopores and micropores of $\mathsf{C a}_{2}\mathsf{A l}_{1}\mathsf{F u}$ [26]. The pore sizes of $\mathrm{Ca}_{2}\mathrm{Al}_{1}\mathrm{Fu}$ shows a micropore at $1.86~\mathrm{{nm}}$ and mesopores at 2.55 and 3.92, and $6.28\ \mathrm{nm}$ , respectively. Meanwhile, the measured BET surface areas were 881, 461, and $734~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for AlFu, CaFu, and $\mathsf{C a}_{2}\mathsf{A l}_{1}\mathsf{F u}$ , respectively (Table S2). Although the surface area value of bimetallic $\mathrm{Ca}_{2}\mathrm{Al}_{1}\mathrm{Fu}$ is lower than that of the pristine AlFu, which can be ascribed to the mesopore production partially sacrificed the pristine MOFs microporous structure, it still surpass pristine CaFu. The high BET surface areas and hierarchically pore sizes are expected to enable bimetallic camellia-like MOFs with outstanding adsorption performance.
## 2.2. Ultrahigh fluoride adsorption and selectivity with hierarchically bimetallic MOFs
Thanks to the high surface areas and porosities, MOFs have been extensively used as adsorbents for contaminant removal [32]. Herein, fluoride from brick tea was examined as the probe contaminant to evaluate the adsorption performance of the hierarchically bimetallic camellia-like $\mathrm{Ca}_{2}\mathrm{Al}_{1}\mathrm{Fu}$ . Considering adsorbent dose is a key influencing factor to evaluate the efficiency of fluoride removal, the optimal bimetallic together with the pristine monometallic MOFs dose were analyzed with brick tea leaves under boiling water for $30\mathrm{min}$ to understand which factor mainly affected fluoride uptake (Fig. 3). As seen, the fluoride adsorption percentage was significantly increased with the dose of MOFs, especially, the bimetallic $\mathsf{C a}_{2}\mathsf{A l}_{1}\mathsf{F u}$ showed the highest adsorption capacity under the same dose conditions among the three adsorbents (Fig. 3a). For pristine monometallic CaFu and AlFu, fluoride removal efficiency increased from $29.8\ \%$ to $43.4\ \%$ and $32\%$ to $46\%$ with frameworks dose increased from 0.4 to $3.2~\mathrm{g/L}$ , respectively. Interestingly, for the bimetallic $\mathsf{C a}_{2}\mathsf{A l}_{1}\mathsf{F u}$ naonochannels, fluoride adsorption obviously increased from $46\%$ to $69\%$ under the same conditions. $\mathsf{C a}_{2}\mathsf{A l}_{1}\mathsf{F u}$ has a higher BET surface area compared to CaFu, but lower than that of AlFu, which demonstrates that the bimetallic Ca and Al nodes of the frameworks with a significant synergistic effect could be the key positive role for the enhanced fluoride adsorption performance. Moreover, the camellia-like nanoflower structure endows $\mathrm{Ca}_{2}\mathrm{Al}_{1}\mathrm{Fu}$ with more binding sites could also be useful to the fluoride immobilization.
Tea polyphenols are the most plentiful natural biological activities and health benefits compounds in tea, have been proved to possess anti-inflammatory and antioxidative bioactivities [33]. Thus, exploiting a simple and highly selective method to removal of fluoride over tea polyphenols in tea is of vital importance to human health. It is noteworthy that when the dose of $\mathsf{C a}_{2}\mathsf{A l}_{1}\mathsf{F u}$ is lower than $1.6~\mathrm{g/L}$ , the fluoride adsorption is maintained high $(66\ \%)$ while tea polyphenols and caffeine were all reduced by lower than $3\%$ (Fig. 3b), indicating that $\mathrm{Ca}_{2}\mathrm{Al}_{1}\mathrm{Fu}$ could highly selective uptake of fluoride over tea polyphenols and caffeine. Such ultrahigh selectivity of $\mathrm{Ca}_{2}\mathrm{Al}_{1}\mathrm{Fu}$ can be attributed to the unique hierarchically camellia-like bimetallic MOFs naonochannels. However, when the dose of $\mathsf{C a}_{2}\mathsf{A l}_{1}\mathsf{F u}$ reaches to $3.2~\mathrm{g/L}$ the fluoride adsorption efficiency increases to $69\%$ , the reduce of tea polyphenols and caffeine increases to about $15\%$ . The CaFu and AlFu adsorbents also show the same trend, which can be seen in Fig. S2. Based on these results, the optimal bimetallic $\mathsf{C a}_{2}\mathsf{A l}_{1}\mathsf{F u}$ dose is fixed at $1.6~\mathrm{g/L}$ for subsequent fluoride removal experiments to ensure both the high adsorption capacity and selectivity.
Given the high removal efficiency and selectivity, fluoride uptake kinetics over CaFu, AlFu and $\mathrm{Ca}_{2}\mathrm{Al}_{1}\mathrm{Fu}$ were also evaluated. In order to accurately study and compare the kinetics, MOFs adsorbents were added to brick tea infusion with $8.5~\mathrm{{mg/L}}$ fluoride concentration. As shown in Fig. 3c, the fluoride removal efficiency increase with the contact time over the three MOFs, among which the monometallic AlFu and CaFu attained an equilibrium state required $15~\mathrm{min}$ , and only need $10~\mathrm{{min}}$ with bimetallic $\mathrm{Ca}_{2}\mathrm{Al}_{1}\mathrm{Fu}$ . The different adsorption time can be ascribed to the different mass transfer kinetics. For $\mathrm{Ca}_{2}\mathrm{Al}_{1}\mathrm{Fu}$ , the hierarchically porous structure shows easier access and diffusion of fluoride. In consideration of the affect of $A1^{3+}$ ions to the human health, the residual ${\mathsf{A l}}^{3+}$ ion concentration of the tea infusion was determined with inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectrometer (ICP-OES). Significantly, there was no residual $A1^{3+}$ ion can be detected in the infusion.
Maximum adsorption capacity of an adsorbent is deemed to be essential for its performance metrics. Here, as-prepared bimetallic $\mathsf{C a}_{2}\mathsf{A l}_{1}\mathsf{F u}$ for maximum fluoride removal was proved by adsorption isotherms with various concentrations of fluoride at 298 K, 308 K and $318\ \mathrm{K},$ respectively. Remarkably, it can be seen from Fig. 4a that an elevating tread of fluoride removal capacity with the increasing of concentration and reached limiting high capacity adsorption at equilibrium of $50~\mathrm{mg/L}$ . Additionly, in order to further quantitatively study the bimetallic MOFs removal behavior, the Langmuir model is used to interprete the results and the obtained correlation coefficient reached 0.9990 (Fig. S6) which indicate that adsorption of fluoride by $\mathsf{C a}_{2}\mathsf{A l}_{1}\mathsf{F u}$ prefer to a single-layer process with homogeneous binding sites [23]. Moreover, according to the Langmuire fitting results, hierarchically bimetallic $\mathrm{Ca}_{2}\mathrm{Al}_{1}\mathrm{Fu}$ adsorbs $51.36~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ of fluoride at $298\mathrm{K}$ from the tea infusion (Table S3). As the temperature further increased, the rapid increase of fluoride adsorption capacity over $\mathrm{Ca}_{2}\mathrm{Al}_{1}\mathrm{Fu}$ occurred. The fluoride uptakes at 308 and $318\mathrm{~K~}$ were measured as 57.20 and $64.68\:\mathrm{\mg}\:\mathrm{\g}^{-1}$ , respectively (Table S3). This phenomenon implies that such fluoride uptake process over $\mathrm{Ca}_{2}\mathrm{Al}_{1}\mathrm{Fu}$ is an endothermic process.
A thermodynamic study was carried out to get a deeper understanding of the ultrahigh fluoride adsorption in $\mathrm{Ca}_{2}\mathrm{Al}_{1}\mathrm{Fu}$ . As illustrated in Table S3, Gibbs free energy change $(\Delta G)$ values on $\mathsf{C a}_{2}\mathsf{A l}_{1}\mathsf{F u}$ at experiment temperatures exhibited negative behavior, indicating spontaneous nature of the brick tea infusion fluoride adsorption process [22]. As seen, $\Delta G$ at the three temperatures over $\mathsf{C a}_{2}\mathsf{A l}_{1}\mathsf{F u}$ are $-2.326$ , $-2.93$ and $-3.673\mathrm{\textmu}\mathrm{\textmu}\mathrm{\mol}^{-1}$ , respectively, indicating that high temperature is beneficial to such fluoride adsorption. To unveil which is the main driving force to affect this spontaneous fluoride adsorption, entropy (DS) and enthalpy change $(\Delta\mathsf{H})$ calculated from Van’t Hoff plot (Fig. S7) are $17.73\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ and $67.29\mathrm{J}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\mathrm{K}^{-1}$ , respectively (Table S3). Notably, the DH of the adsorption process over $\mathsf{C a}_{2}\mathsf{A l}_{1}\mathsf{F u}$ on fluoride exhibits a positive value, which represents an endothermic reaction [35]. This result further highlights the advantage of high temperature for the adsorption capacity. In line with this, the positive DS value shows that the randomness increases during the fluoride uptake by $\mathrm{Ca}_{2}\mathrm{Al}_{1}\mathrm{Fu}$ . These observations clearly verify that the main driving force is $\Delta S$ during such fluoride removal by $\mathrm{Ca}_{2}\mathrm{Al}_{1}\mathrm{Fu}$ .
Since the different metal molar ratio of the bimetallic MOFs is sensitive to the adsorption performance, a series of CaAlFu with different $\mathsf{C a}/\mathsf{A l}$ molar ratios were synthesized and compared the fluoride adsorption capacities. As seen, the capacity for fluoride uptake by CaAlFu gradually increased when the molar ratio of $\mathsf{C a}/\mathsf{A l}$ increase at 2:1 and then displayed a downward trend (Fig. S8). Obviously, the monometallic CaFu $16.3\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1},$ ) and AlFu $(24.3\mathrm{\mg\9^{-1}},$ ) shows a lower fluoride adsorption capacity compared to bimetallic CaAlFu, suggesting that a synergistic effect between the two metal elements can remarkably improve the adsorption capacity. These results demonstrate that there should be an optimal molar ratio of Ca and Al in bimetallic CaAlFu adsorbent.
Regeneration and cyclability performance of an adsorbent are considered to be important indicators, not only due to markedly affect adsorption efficiency but also reduce the production cost. Owing to the bimetallic $\mathsf{C a}_{2}\mathsf{A l}_{1}\mathsf{F u}$ exhibits the best fluoride adsorption capacity, we chose this adsorbent to evaluate the regeneration and reusability performance of CaAlFu. The fluoride adsorbed $\mathbf{Ca}_{2}.$ - ${\sf A l}_{1}$ Fu can be efficiently regenerated by eluting with NaOH solution $(0.2~\mathrm{M})$ . As shown in Fig. 4b, compared with the initial $\mathsf{C a}_{2}\mathsf{A l}_{1}\mathsf{F u}$ , the regenerated bimetallic MOF still maintains a high adsorption efficiency of $62\%$ after 5 cycles, with only $4\%$ reduction for fluoride from tea infusion. Such only insignificant loss in the fluoride adsorption, which may be due to the loss of adsorbent during each round of adsorption–desorption process. Additionly, PXRD pattern of the hierarchical $\mathrm{Ca}_{2}\mathrm{Al}_{1}\mathrm{Fu}$ after the five consecutive reuses shows no significant changes compared with the sample used before (Fig. S9), demonstrating that the hierarchical bimetallic MOFs possess excellent stability under this adsorption conditions. Moreover, the SEM image of the $\mathsf{C a}_{2}\mathsf{A l}_{1}\mathsf{F u}$ after adsorption show no obvious changes in morphology (Fig. S10a). According to the EDX investigation (Fig. S10b), C, O, Ca, Al and F elements can be detected after the fluoride adsorption.
Considering the $\mathsf{C a}_{2}\mathsf{A l}_{1}$ -node of the framework and type of fluoride present in the water are sensitive to $\mathsf{p H}$ , the adsorption performance of the adsorbent for fluoride in brick tea infusion was verified at different pH from 2 to 12. It is observed from Fig. 4c, $\mathrm{Ca}_{2}\mathrm{Al}_{1}\mathrm{Fu}$ displayed high fluoride uptake efficiency at pH range from 2 to 7, and subsequently gradual attenuation was observed. Since the brick tea infusion $\mathsf{p H}$ of this study is 5–6, the pH was not adjusted in the tea matrix adsorption experiment, which was more promising for applications. Significantly, the brick tea infusion has a lower fluoride concentration compared to the permissible limit of WHO drinking standard after adsorption $(1.24~\mathrm{mg/L})$ [23]. Further, the zeta potential of $\mathsf{C a}_{2}\mathsf{A l}_{1}\mathsf{F u}$ under different pH values were evaluated (Fig. S11). The PZC of the bimetallic adsorbent is around 8.3, as pH increases from 8 to 12, the $\mathsf{C a}_{2}\mathsf{A l F u}$ becomes more negatively charged, which indicates that electrostatic repulsion could promoted between the negatively charged surface of $\mathsf{C a}_{2}\mathsf{A l}_{1}\mathsf{F u}$ and fluoride under basic condition.
Moreover, the complicated co-existing anions in the brick tea infusion would also interfere the fluoride remove efficiency by MOFs, the selectivity of fluoride over various other anions such as Cl-, $\mathsf{P O}_{4}^{3-}$ , $S0_{4}^{2-}$ , ${\mathsf{N O}}_{3}^{-}$ and ${\sf H C O}_{3}^{-}$ with 8.5 and $272{\mathrm{mg/L}}$ in $\mathsf{C a}_{2}\mathsf{A l}_{1}\mathsf{F u}$ hierarchical nanochannel was carried out. As shown in Fig. 4d that even when the concentration of Cl-, ${\mathsf{N O}}^{3-}$ and $S0_{4}^{2-}$ is 32 times than fluoride, $\mathrm{Ca}_{2}\mathrm{Al}_{1}\mathrm{Fu}$ also showed negligible effect on the fluoride removal efficiency from the mixture anion solutions. Meanwhile, ${\mathrm{HCO}}_{3}^{-}$ and $\mathsf{P O}_{4}^{3-}$ had a slight effect on the fluoride removal with only reduced by approximately $16.7\%$ and $21.6\%$ (at $272{\mathrm{~mg/L}} $ , respectively. Although ${\sf H C O}_{3}^{-}$ and $\mathsf{P O}_{4}^{3-}$ had a slight competitive effect on the fluoride adsorption, the concentration of ${\sf H C O}_{3}^{-}$ and $\mathsf{P O}_{4}^{3-}$ in tea infusion is usually very low. These comprehensive results show that the $\mathsf{C a}_{2}\mathsf{A l}_{1}\mathsf{F u}$ possesses an excellent selectively of fluoride to the other co-existing anions in the tea infusion.
## 2.3. Sensory evaluation of tea sample
In order to check whether the adsorption process affects the chemical profile and aroma composition of the tea, the color and sensory quality of the brick tea before and after adsorption process with different hierarchically $\mathrm{Ca}_{2}\mathrm{Al}_{1}\mathrm{Fu}$ dose were compared (Fig. 5) [36]. The tea sensory evaluation is valued by an expert panel (Table S1).
Fig. 5 shows the differences in aroma, liquor color, and taste of steeped leaves at different adsorbent doses. The color of the tea infusions and leaves after the adsorption show no obvious change as compared to those without adding adsorbent. In addition, when the dose of $\mathsf{C a}_{2}\mathsf{A l}_{1}\mathsf{F u}$ is lower than $1.6~\mathrm{g/L}$ , the tea infusion condition retain as similar as before adsorption with the score of aroma range from 1 to 4 and taste from 3 to 4 (Table S4). However, when the dose of $\mathsf{C a}_{2}\mathsf{A l}_{1}\mathsf{F u}$ reaches to $3.2~\mathrm{g/L}$ , the score of the infusion taste was reduced to $^{-1}$ . At the same time, the tea infusion aroma score was also reduced to $^{-1}$ . These changes may result from the adsorption of phenolic compounds by the high MOFs dose, which is consistent with the previous high performance liquid chromatography (HPLC) analysis. Thus, there should be an optimal dose of $\mathrm{Ca}_{2}\mathrm{Al}_{1}\mathrm{Fu}$ during the fluoride adsorption from tea.
## 2.4. Mechanism of fluoride adsorption in $C a_{2}A l_{1}F u$ channels
To further investigate the ultrahigh fluoride adsorption mechanism in hierarchical $\mathsf{C a}_{2}\mathsf{A l}_{1}\mathsf{F u}$ nanochannels, the surface chemical state of the adsorbents was investigated by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) (Fig. 6). The survey spectroscopy confirms bimetallic $\mathrm{Ca}_{2}\mathrm{Al}_{1}\mathrm{Fu}$ is composed of Ca, Al, O, and C peaks (Fig. 6a), which is agree with the previous SEM-EDX analysis. After adsorption of fluoride ions, apart from those peaks as before adsorption, F1 s appeared at around 685.1 eV can be detected, confirming the fluoride was successfully adsorbed by $\mathrm{Ca}_{2}\mathrm{Al}_{1}\mathrm{Fu}$ [37]. The Ca 2p bimodal peaks at 346.9 and $350.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ for $\mathrm{Ca}_{2}\mathrm{Al}_{1}\mathrm{Fu}$ are attributed to Ca $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and Ca $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ (Fig. 6c), which is similar with the previous reports [30]. Meanwhile, the binding peak of Al1 s around at 73.1 eV (Fig. 6d) is characteristic of ${\mathsf{A l}}^{3+}$ ions [8]. It is noteworthy that the $\mathsf{C a}^{2+}2\mathsf{p}$ binding energy in bimetallic $\mathrm{Ca}_{2}\mathrm{Al}_{1}\mathrm{Fu}$ was shifted towards the higher binding position as compared to the monometallic CaFu $345.9\ \mathrm{eV}$ for $\mathsf{C a}^{2+}2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ , $349.4\ \mathrm{eV}$ for ${\mathsf{C a}}^{2+}$ $\mathsf{2p}_{1/2}\rangle$ . Contrarily, $A|^{3+}1$ s binding energy for $\mathrm{Ca}_{2}\mathrm{Al}_{1}\mathrm{Fu}$ was shifted towards the lower binding energy position as compared to the monometallic AlFu $(73.5~\mathrm{eV})$ . The results indicate that there may be a relatively weaker interaction force between the ${\mathsf{C a}}^{2+}$ and ${\mathbb{A}}^{3+}$ in the bimetallic MOFs, which contributes to the improvement of the fluoride adsorption performance. What is interesting is that both the Ca 2p ( $347.4\mathrm{eV}$ for $\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ , $351.0\mathrm{eV}$ for $\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}2\mathsf{p}_{1/2})$ and ${\mathsf{A l}}^{3+}$ 1 s $(74.7\ \mathrm{{\eV})}$ binding energies in $\mathsf{C a}_{2}\mathsf{A l}_{1}\mathsf{F u}$ after fluoride adsorption becomes slightly higher as compared to that before adsorption, proving that Ca and Al coordination environment of the bimetallic MOFs has changed with fluoride uptake. This was further confirmed by the F1 s spectrum (Fig. 6 b). Notably, the F 1 s at 684.8 and $686.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ could be attributed to the Ca-F $63.5\ \%$ , molar percent) and Al-F $36.5\%$ , molar percent), respectively. Such two kinds of fluoride bonds content after fluoride adsorption is consistent with the molar ratio of $\mathsf{C a}/\mathsf{A l}$ in the bimetallic MOF.
The O1 s spectra show three peaks including metal oxides $(0^{2-})$ metals-OH and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (Fig. 6e and f) [29]. Compared with the initial adsorbent, the percentage of $-{\mathsf{O H}}$ group decreased from $68.31\%$ to $60.52\%$ after the fluoride adsorption of $\mathsf{C a}_{2}\mathsf{A l}_{1}\mathsf{F u}$ , suggesting that the –OH groups were partially substituted by fluoride ions during such adsorption process. Moreover, from Fourier transform infrared spectrometer (FT-IR) spectrum of $\mathrm{Ca}_{2}\mathrm{Al}_{1}\mathrm{Fu}$ (Fig. S12), the bands near $3465~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $3560~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ of the $\mathrm{Ca}_{2}\mathrm{Al}_{1}\mathrm{Fu}$ correspond to Ca and Al bimetallic terminal $-\mathsf{O H}$ group is remarkably diminished after fluoride uptake respectively [29]. Meanwhile, after uptake, the small bands at $2745~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $740~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ correspond to hydrogen-bonding between $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ and CaAl-OH nodes disappeared completely [31]. Based on these results, it is indicated that the Ca and Al coordinatively unsaturated centers in $\mathrm{Ca}_{2}\mathrm{Al}_{1}\mathrm{Fu}$ play a key role during the adsorption, wherein fluoride ions coordinated with metal nodes of the MOFs by replacing –OH group.
To gain a deeper molecular level insight, theoretical calculations were conducted to understand the adsorption mechanism over the hierarchical bimetallic MOFs. Here, we performed density functional theory (DFT) methods using the DMOL3 module to calculate the adsorption energy of fluoride ions, hydroxyl groups, and tea polyphenol on $\mathrm{Ca}_{2}\mathrm{Al}_{1}\mathrm{Fu}$ . As shown in Fig. 7a, the adsorption energy of fluoride on $\mathrm{Ca}_{2}\mathrm{Al}_{1}\mathrm{Fu}$ was $-0.20\ \mathrm{~eV}$ , suggesting that a strong interaction between the fluoride and frameworks. In addition, the adsorption energy of –OH group and tea polyphenol over $\mathrm{Ca}_{2}\mathrm{Al}_{1}\mathrm{Fu}$ was calculated to be $-0.10\ \mathrm{eV}$ (Fig. 7b) and $-0.004\ \mathrm{eV}$ (Fig. 7c), respectively, suggesting that the preferential adsorption of fluoride on the hierarchical bimetallic MOFs compared with $-\mathsf{O H}$ group and tea polyphenol. These results demonstrate that the selective fluoride removal through anion exchange using $\mathsf{C a}_{2}\mathsf{A l}_{1}$ Fu was a feasible way.
All these results manifest that hierarchical bimetallic camellialike $\mathrm{Ca}_{2}\mathrm{Al}_{1}\mathrm{Fu}$ adsorbent exhibits outstanding performance for boosting selective fluoride removal from the tea. Combined with previous reports [21], a plausible fluoride removal mechanism by $\mathsf{C a}_{2}\mathsf{A l}_{1}\mathsf{F u}$ is reported (Fig. 7d). The DFT results demonstrate that the fluoride was preferably adsorbed onto bimetallic $\mathsf{C a}_{2}\mathsf{A l}_{1}\mathsf{F u}$ , while –OH group can spontaneously be dissociated from the bimetallic framework. Hence, firstly, fluoride is adsorbed into $\mathbf{C}\mathbf{a}_{2}$ - $\mathsf{A l}_{1}\mathsf{F}\mathsf{u}$ hierarchical nanochannels, which increases the contact probability between fluoride and bimetallic ${\mathsf{C a}}_{2}{\mathsf{A l}}_{1}$ center of MOFs. Then, the electron transfers from fluoride ions to the $\mathrm{Ca}_{2}\mathrm{Al}_{1}\mathrm{Fu}$ and subsequently the hydroxyl groups adsorbed on the bimetallic center takes electrons. Finally, the hydroxyl groups dissociated from the adsorbent via the anion exchange behavior. In contrast, tea polyphenols cannot replaces hydroxyl groups of $\mathrm{Ca}_{2}\mathrm{Al}_{1}\mathrm{Fu}$ due to their larger diameter and lower adsorption energy $(-0.004~\mathrm{eV})$ than that of hydroxyl groups. Therefore, a highly selectivity adsorption of the fluoride over tea polyphenols in tea over the hierarchical bimetallic $\mathrm{Ca}_{2}\mathrm{Al}_{1}\mathrm{Fu}$ can be obtained.
## 3. Conclusions
In summary, this study for the first time develops an in situ bimetal competitive coordination combined with alkalineassisted strategy for fabrication hierarchical camellia-like MOFs as an outstanding fluoride binding affinity from brick tea. Attributing to the pre-addition of sodium hydroxide during the synthesis of the bimetallic MOFs, the resulting suitable camellia-like $\mathrm{Ca}_{2}\mathrm{Al}_{1}\mathrm{Fu}$ hierarchical structure is constructed, which endows the framework with outstanding fluoride binding selectivity over tea polyphenols. Detailed kinetics and thermodynamic studies suggest that the unique camellia-like morphology and hierarchically bimetallic structure of the $\mathrm{Ca}_{2}\mathrm{Al}_{1}\mathrm{Fu}$ is vital for highly efficient for fluoride adsorption. Moreover, experiments and DFT theoretical calculations reveal the details on fluoride and CaAl low-coordination active centers in the bimetallic MOFs strong interaction that result in such outstanding selective fluoride uptake performance. Additionally, the sensory evaluation for satisfactory adsorption capacity ensures the practical application ability of $\mathsf{C a}_{2}\mathsf{A l}_{1}\mathsf{F u}$ for fluoride adsorption from tea. The simple but efficient strategy of this study will give a spark of inspiration for constructing hierarchical bimetallic-based MOFs for practical fluoride decontamination from tea and water worldwide.
